        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Battle of Marengo</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">14 June 1800</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val">On a plain east of Alessandria in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, between Marengo village, Castel Ceriolo and San Giuliano Vecchio, with the Fontanone stream between Marengo and the river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bormida">Bormida</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> under the First <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">Consul</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a>, with the Army of the Reserve officially commanded by Louis Alexandre Berthier: 22,000 men and 15 guns at first, with 6,000 more when Desaix arrived. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> under General Michael von Melas: 30,000 men and 100 guns</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Austrians, who had lost about half the forces they had committed, fell back into Alessandria. Within 24 hours Melas began negotiations that led to the Convention of Alessandria and the Austrian evacuation of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ticino">Ticino</a>.</span></div>
