    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">May 1800</span><span class="rf-text">Bonaparte leads his Army of the Reserve over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">June 1800</span><span class="rf-text">The French seize <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and cut the main Austrian supply route along the south bank of the Po</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">June 1800</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> surrenders to the Austrians, freeing a large number of them for operations against the French</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">June 1800</span><span class="rf-text">Lannes defeats Ott at Montebello</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">June 1800</span><span class="rf-text">The Austrians attack across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bormida">Bormida</a> and break the French centre by early afternoon</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">June 1800</span><span class="rf-text">Desaix's arrival and Kellermann's cavalry charge turn the battle, and the Austrians are routed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">June 1800</span><span class="rf-text">Melas begins negotiations that lead to the Convention of Alessandria</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1805</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> holds a ceremony on the field to lay the founding stone of a pyramid</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-marengo" data-v="c7f7ff949e" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The two armies take their ground</b> The Austrians crossed the Bormida in three bodies. Victor held Marengo and the Fontanone with Kellermann's cavalry on his left, Lannes stood to the north-east, and Monnier and the Guard waited east of Castel Ceriolo.</li><li><b>The Austrian assaults at the Fontanone fail</b> Melas sent his divisions at Marengo across the stream one after another. Forced into a funnel by the ground, the first attack failed under fire from two sides, and the second was beaten off by 11 am.</li><li><b>Ott takes Castel Ceriolo and presses the French right</b> Ott's leading troops took Castel Ceriolo and began to press the French right flank. Monnier's division and the Guard were brought across to extend and shore up that flank.</li><li><b>The Austrians bridge the Fontanone and take Stortiglione</b> A small bridge was thrown over the Fontanone under cover of reserve artillery, and Lattermann's grenadiers crossed to attack the French defending Marengo village. O'Reilly took Stortiglione by 2:00 pm.</li><li><b>Victor withdraws to the vines</b> Victor could no longer hold his positions and fell back south-east to the main belt of vines, with Lannes mirroring the move.</li><li><b>Ott defeats Monnier</b> Ott, aided by Frimont, defeated Monnier's division and forced two thirds of it to retreat to the north-east, away from the main French army.</li><li><b>The Consular Guard is destroyed</b> Bonaparte sent his main Guard battalion and its artillery to flank the Austrian column and delay it. After a firefight of fifteen minutes the Guard was surprised and destroyed by Frimont's cavalry.</li><li><b>The French fall back towards San Giuliano</b> The French fell back about 3 km and tried to regroup at San Giuliano. The Austrian centre formed into a pursuit column to chase them off the field.</li><li><b>Desaix's force arrives and meets the column</b> Boudet's division was deployed in front of San Giuliano and advanced in line of brigades against the head of the Austrian column, defeating its leading brigade. The column came on along the road.</li><li><b>Lattermann's grenadiers renew the attack</b> The Austrian staff brought up Lattermann's grenadier brigade in line and renewed the attack, with a dragoon regiment in support.</li><li><b>Desaix is sent forward and the cavalry is ordered in</b> Faced with a crisis, Napoleon sent Desaix forward again and ordered the cavalry charge that Desaix had asked for.</li><li><b>Kellermann's charge breaks the grenadiers</b> Lattermann's formation was charged on its left flank by Kellermann's heavy cavalry, about 400 men, and disintegrated.</li><li><b>The supporting dragoons are routed</b> Murat and Kellermann at once attacked the supporting Liechtenstein Dragoons, which were too slow to respond, and routed them. The fleeing horsemen crashed into Pilatti's troopers and carried them away.</li><li><b>The Austrian main body breaks and the French advance</b> As the terrified cavalry stampeded past, the exhausted Austrian infantry of the main body lost heart and rushed to the rear. The whole French infantry line advanced westward.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/andre-massena"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/andre-massena.jpg" alt="André Masséna" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/andre-massena">André Masséna</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Andr%C3%A9_Mass%C3%A9na_(Fontaine_et_Gros).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Edme-Adolphe Fontaine / After Antoine-Jean Gros, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle was the last act of Bonaparte's Italian campaign of 1800. In mid-May he took his Army of the Reserve, officially commanded by Louis Alexandre Berthier, over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> almost before the passes were open, crossing on a mule, and threatened the lines of communication of the Austrian commander Michael von Melas in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. The French seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> on 2 June, followed by Pavia, Piacenza and Stradella, which cut the main Austrian supply route eastward along the south bank of the Po. Bonaparte hoped that Melas would be kept busy with the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>, held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andre-massena">André Masséna</a>, but Genoa surrendered on 4 June and freed a large number of Austrians.</p><p>On 9 June Jean Lannes beat Feldmarschallleutnant Peter Ott at Montebello. Bonaparte became overconfident and convinced that Melas would not attack and that the Austrians were about to retreat. Melas had withdrawn most of his troops from their positions near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a> and Genoa to Alessandria on the main Turin-Mantua road. The Austrians planned to fight their way out eastward, but used a local double agent, usually known by his cover of François Toli, to make Bonaparte think they would march north, cross the Po and head for Milan.</p><p>Bonaparte knew that Ott had no way out from Alessandria but had no idea of Melas's position. Fearing that the Austrian general might escape, he spread his army in a wide net, sending Louis Desaix with Jean Boudet's division (6,000 men) south to Novi Ligure and La Poype's division (3,500 men) north on the other bank of the Po.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Austrians had 30,000 men and 100 guns, at first opposed by 22,000 French with 15 guns; the arrival of Desaix added 6,000 men to Bonaparte's army. The Austrian army had been worn down by the 1799 campaign, with casualties and disease reducing some regiments to 300 men, and only about 15% of the reinforcements sent from distant home bases reached the field army. Melas split it into three corps facing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bormida">Bormida</a> in front of Alessandria: Ott in the north, with Gottesheim's advance guard and the divisions of Schellenberg and Vogelsang; O'Reilly's division in the south; and Melas himself in the centre, with the divisions of Hadik, Kaim, Morzin and Elsnitz.</p><p>The 36,000 French troops in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1799 had been in a desperate state, with supplies inadequate and discipline breaking down. For the Army of the Reserve Bonaparte first overhauled the supply system. Its core was 30,000 men, mostly from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavian-republic">Batavian Republic</a>, who had been used under Brune to crush the rebellion in the Vendée, with veterans from the remains of the former Army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. The new doctrine stressed the offensive, mobility and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonet">bayonet</a> over linear firepower.</p><p>The plain east of Alessandria was crossed by the meandering Bormida, over which the Austrians held a bridgehead, and was dotted with hamlets and farms. Marengo lay to the west, Castel Ceriolo to the north and San Giuliano Vecchio to the east, with the small Fontanone stream between Marengo and the Bormida. Victor's corps stood in and south of Marengo, Lannes's to the north-east, and Monnier's division and the Guard, the reserve, east of Castel Ceriolo.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Austrians crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bormida">Bormida</a> by two bridges into a narrow bend, and poor staff work meant the whole army had to file through a narrow bridgehead. The movement began about 6 am, the first shots came around 8 am and the attack was fully developed by 9 am. Melas's centre, about 18,000 men, was held at the Fontanone by Gardanne's division, and attacks by the divisions of Hadik and Kaim failed by 11 am, Hadik being killed. Ott's 7,500 men on the left took Castel Ceriolo around 11:30 am.</p><p>Bonaparte, 5 kilometres away, took until about 10 am to see that this was no diversion and was on the field by 11. Around 12:30 pm the Austrians bridged the Fontanone, and Victor, unable to hold, withdrew south-east, with Lannes mirroring him. By 2:30 pm the French were withdrawing and Austrian dragoons seized the Marengo farm. Bonaparte committed his main Guard battalion against Schellenberg's column, and after a 15-minute firefight around 4:00 pm it was surprised and destroyed by Frimont's cavalry. Melas, slightly wounded and 71, handed command to his chief of staff Anton von Zach and Kaim,.</p><p>Desaix reported to Bonaparte shortly before 5:00 pm with 6,000 men and 9 guns of Boudet's division. The 9th Light Infantry delayed the column for 30 minutes, Marmont's guns fired grapeshot, and Kellermann's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a>, about 400 men, charged Lattermann's grenadiers on the flank, which disintegrated. Desaix was shot from his horse and Zach and at least 2,000 of his men were taken. Murat and Kellermann routed the Liechtenstein Dragoons, and the Austrian infantry lost heart.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Austrians fell back into Alessandria, having lost about half the forces they had committed in 12 hours of fighting: 15 colours, 40 guns, almost 8,000 taken prisoner and 6,500 dead or wounded. French casualties were on the order of 4,700 killed and wounded and 900 missing or captured, but they kept the battlefield and the strategic initiative. Desaix's body was found among the slain. A last defence around Marengo village by Weidenfeld's second grenadier brigade, O'Reilly's returning cavalry and Frimont let the Austrian centre reach safety behind the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bormida">Bormida</a> as night fell, and Ott fought his way back to the bridgehead.</p><p>Bonaparte needed to leave for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> urgently and the next morning sent Berthier to the Austrian headquarters. Within 24 hours Melas began the negotiations of the Convention of Alessandria, which led to the Austrians evacuating north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ticino">Ticino</a> and suspending military operations in Italy.</p><p>The outcome strengthened Bonaparte's position as First <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">Consul</a> after his coup of the previous November. Generals who had been hostile to him saw that his luck had not abandoned him, and he had done what Schérer, Joubert, Championnet and even Moreau had not, inflicting a decisive blow on the Coalition. He rejected offers from the Count of Provence, who had regarded the Consulate as a transition towards restoring the king. Moreau's victory at Hohenlinden in fact ended the war, and Bonaparte played it down.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/napoleon.jpg" alt="Napoleon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jacques-Louis_David_-_The_Emperor_Napoleon_in_His_Study_at_the_Tuileries_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jacques-Louis David, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Accounts of the battle come largely from the commanders and officers on both sides and from what Bonaparte's government made of it. Melas wrote to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> that evening that Kellermann's charge had broken his soldiers, and Murat told Berthier that Kellermann's charge tipped the balance. In the army bulletin issued the next day, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> set against it a charge by Bessières at the head of the Guard grenadiers. The battle was then mythologised in that bulletin and three increasingly glamourised 'Official Reports' during Bonaparte's reign, and tales were invented about the Guard and the 72nd demibrigade.</p><p>Berthier's Relation de la bataille de Marengo, published in 1804, presented the retreat as a calculated movement to give Desaix time to take position. Desaix's arrival was expected but not certain before the retreat, and the bulletin's claim that his force waited in reserve is false, since it arrived late. Marmont, Captain Coignet, Captain Gervais and General Thévenet all describe an army that was close to being beaten, with Coignet writing that they were 'all ready to start running at the earliest sign of danger'. Desaix's remark that the battle was lost rests only on a story.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterloo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterloo.jpg" alt="Battle of Waterloo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterloo">Battle of Waterloo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Waterloo_1815.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">William Sadler, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Marengo is called a political victory that secured Bonaparte's grip on power after the coup, and a last-gasp victory in reality. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> sought to ensure it would not be forgotten. On 5 May 1805 he and Joséphine presided over a ceremony on the field where General Chasseloup gave him the founding stone of a pyramid, part of a project for a 'city of Victories' whose boulevards, named after Italian battles, would converge on it. The project was abandoned in 1815 and the stones were recovered by peasants. A column erected in 1801 was removed and restored in 1922.</p><p>Several ships of the French Navy were named Marengo, and in 1802 a Marengo <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> was named in honour of the battle. Napoleon's mount at the battle was also called Marengo and carried him at Austerlitz, Jena-Auerstedt, Wagram and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterloo">Waterloo</a>. Marengo County in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a>, first settled by Napoleonic refugees with their Vine and Olive Colony, was named for the battle, as were many settlements in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><p>The dish chicken Marengo is named for the victory, and its recipe is generally credited to Dunan, chef to Napoleon's army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, who made it in haste that evening. Puccini's Tosca of 1900, set in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> a hundred years earlier, uses the battle: reports of an Austrian victory are corrected by news that Napoleon won, and Cavaradossi cries 'Vittoria!'. A museum of the battle, the Museo della Battaglia di Marengo, stands at Spinetta Marengo, and re-enactments are held every year.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>At Marengo on 14 June 1800 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a>'s French army, nearly beaten by Melas's Austrians, was saved by Desaix's arrival and Kellermann's cavalry charge, and drove them back into Alessandria. Within 24 hours the Austrians began talks that took them out of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</em></p>

